# Tabular Data Classification on Imbalanced Data

Predicting a rare event (customer churn, ~5% positives) from messy tabular data.
Stack: **Python, Pandas, Scikit-Learn, imbalanced-learn, XGBoost, CatBoost**.

## What it does

1. **Preprocessing (Pandas + Scikit-Learn `ColumnTransformer`)**
   - Numerics: median imputation (+ missing-value indicator columns) -> `StandardScaler`
   - Categoricals: constant imputation -> `OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown="ignore")`
   - CatBoost uses its native categorical handling instead of one-hot
2. **Class imbalance** - compared head to head:
   - no handling (baseline)
   - class weights (`class_weight`, `scale_pos_weight`, `auto_class_weights`)
   - SMOTE oversampling and random undersampling (applied inside an `imblearn` pipeline, so only the training split is resampled - no leakage)
3. **Models** - Logistic Regression baseline, XGBoost, CatBoost
4. **Evaluation (business-first, not accuracy)**
   - Stratified train / validation / test split (60/20/20)
   - Decision threshold tuned on the **validation** set (F1 or a cost function), then applied once to the test set
   - Precision, Recall, F1, PR-AUC, ROC-AUC, and a **business cost** (missed churner = 100, wasted retention offer = 10; configurable)

## Run it

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
python src/make_data.py                 # writes data/customers.csv (synthetic, 40k rows, 5% positive)
python src/pipeline.py                  # trains all 8 configurations, writes outputs/
python src/pipeline.py --threshold-metric cost --cost-fn 200 --cost-fp 10   # optimize business cost instead
```

Outputs: `outputs/results.csv`, `pr_curves.png`, `confusion_matrix.png`, `feature_importance.png`.

## Use a real dataset (recommended before putting this on a resume)

The synthetic data lets the project run offline, but real data is more convincing. Good imbalanced options:
Telco Customer Churn (~27% positive), Bank Marketing (~12%), Credit Card Fraud (~0.17%), Give Me Some Credit (~7%).

```bash
python src/pipeline.py --data data/your_file.csv --target <target_column> --id-cols <id_column>
```

The target must be 0/1. Everything else (column types, missing values) is detected automatically.

## Results on the synthetic data (test set, threshold tuned on validation by F1)

| Model | Imbalance strategy | Precision | Recall | F1 | PR-AUC |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| CatBoost | class weights | 0.38 | 0.35 | 0.36 | 0.33 |
| XGBoost | class weights | 0.38 | 0.34 | 0.36 | 0.33 |
| XGBoost | none | 0.32 | 0.39 | 0.35 | 0.32 |
| LogReg | class weights | 0.35 | 0.34 | 0.34 | 0.28 |
| XGBoost | SMOTE | 0.29 | 0.37 | 0.33 | 0.28 |

Always predicting "no churn" scores **95% accuracy** with zero recall - that is why accuracy is not used.
Gradient boosting beats the linear baseline mainly on PR-AUC (non-linear interactions), but gaps between
imbalance strategies are small and within noise for a single split; check with cross-validation on real data
before claiming one strategy wins.

## Ideas to extend it

- Stratified k-fold cross-validation and confidence intervals on the metrics
- Hyperparameter tuning with Optuna (optimize PR-AUC or cost)
- Probability calibration (`CalibratedClassifierCV`) before threshold selection
- SHAP values to explain predictions
- Feature engineering (ratios such as charges per month of tenure, frequency encodings)